them. \code{selected} in the coefficient table is that answer.

\code{penalty} names a corner of one model rather than three different models.
\code{alpha} is the weight on the L1 penalty against the L2 one, so \code{"lasso"} is
\code{alpha = 1}, \code{"ridge"} is \code{alpha = 0}, and \code{"elastic_net"} is everything
between, \code{alpha} then being tuned like \code{lambda}. The first two ignore the
\code{alpha} argument, and \code{parameters$alpha} reports what was used either way.

\describe{
\item{Cross-validation chooses the model here, and does not merely score
it}{This is the one place where this family departs from
\code{\link[=fit_linear_regression]{fit_linear_regression()}}, where \code{cv} decides nothing about the fit.
\code{lambda} and \code{alpha} are chosen by the resampled metric, which is \code{RMSE}
for a regression and \code{Accuracy} for a classification, and the final model
is then fitted on all usable rows at that pair. \code{performance} therefore
holds one row per candidate, and \code{parameters$alpha} and
\code{parameters$lambda} are the pair that won. With \code{cv = FALSE} there is
nothing to choose between, so the grid must name exactly one candidate.}
\item{The estimates have no standard error, and the table says so by not
having the column}{A penalized estimate is deliberately biased, and the
usual standard error assumes an unbiased one, so there is no honest number
to put under \code{stderr}, \code{statistic}, \code{df}, \code{pval} or the confidence limits.
The table carries only what a penalized fit answers — \code{estimate} and
\code{selected} — rather than those six columns filled with \code{NA} down their
whole length, which would read as a table that lost its values instead of a
model that never had them. \code{is.null(fit$coefficients$pval)} is how a
consumer tells the two kinds of table apart. Use
\code{\link[=fit_linear_regression]{fit_linear_regression()}} or \code{\link[=fit_logistic_regression]{fit_logistic_regression()}} when the p-value
is the point.}
\item{The predictors are penalized on a standardised scale}{\code{glmnet}
standardises each column before penalizing it, since otherwise a predictor
measured in millimetres would be shrunk less than the same predictor in
metres, and returns the coefficients on their original scale. They are
therefore comparable with each other in what they mean per unit, but not
with an unpenalized coefficient of the same predictor, which was not
shrunk at all.}
